window.READING=[{"title": "The Hidden Life of Urban Bees", "topic": "Environment / Biology", "paras": [{"l": "A", "t": "For most of the twentieth century, cities were regarded by ecologists as biological deserts: expanses of concrete and asphalt in which only a handful of hardy species, such as pigeons and rats, could survive. Insects that depend on flowering plants were thought to be among the first casualties of urban expansion. Yet over the past two decades a growing body of research has challenged this assumption. Surveys carried out in cities as different as Berlin, Melbourne and Toronto have revealed populations of wild bees that are not only surviving but, in some cases, appear to be more diverse than those found in the surrounding countryside."}, {"l": "B", "t": "The explanation for this apparent paradox lies largely in the transformation of modern agriculture. Since the 1950s, farmland in many developed countries has been consolidated into vast fields planted with a single crop. Hedgerows, which once provided nesting sites and a succession of wild flowers, have been removed to make way for larger machinery, and herbicides have eliminated many of the so-called weeds on which pollinators feed. As a result, a bee foraging in an intensively farmed landscape may encounter an abundance of food for a few weeks while the crop is in bloom, followed by months of virtual starvation. Cities, by contrast, offer a patchwork of private gardens, parks, railway embankments and abandoned plots, where plants flower in sequence from early spring until late autumn."}, {"l": "C", "t": "It would be misleading, however, to suggest that urban environments are uniformly hospitable. Dr Hana Kowalczyk, an entomologist who has studied bee communities in Central European cities for fifteen years, points out that the benefits are unevenly distributed. 'Species that nest in hollow stems or in the cavities of old walls do remarkably well,' she explains, 'but ground-nesting bees, which make up roughly seventy per cent of all species, struggle wherever soil is sealed beneath paving.' Her surveys suggest that the composition of urban bee populations is therefore skewed towards particular groups, even when the total number of species is high."}, {"l": "D", "t": "A further complication is the rapid growth of urban beekeeping. Encouraged by media campaigns that present the honeybee as a symbol of environmental health, thousands of city dwellers have installed hives on rooftops and balconies. In London, the number of registered colonies more than doubled between 2008 and 2018. Ecologists have expressed concern that this enthusiasm may be counterproductive. Honeybees are generalist foragers that live in colonies of up to fifty thousand individuals, and a high density of hives can deplete the nectar and pollen on which smaller, solitary wild species depend. Some researchers have also warned that managed colonies can transmit viral diseases to wild populations, although the extent of this risk remains disputed."}, {"l": "E", "t": "Recognising these tensions, several municipal authorities have begun to adopt policies aimed explicitly at wild pollinators rather than honeybees. The city of Utrecht in the Netherlands has converted the roofs of more than three hundred bus shelters into miniature meadows planted with sedum and other drought-tolerant species. Elsewhere, councils have reduced the frequency with which roadside verges are mown, allowing native flowers to set seed. Such measures are relatively inexpensive, but their effectiveness is difficult to measure, since bee populations fluctuate considerably from year to year in response to weather conditions."}, {"l": "F", "t": "To address this problem, a number of projects now rely on volunteers to collect data. In one long-running scheme, members of the public are asked to spend ten minutes observing a patch of flowers and to record the number and type of insects that visit it. Critics have questioned whether untrained observers can reliably distinguish between closely related species, many of which can only be identified under a microscope. The organisers acknowledge this limitation but argue that the sheer volume of observations compensates for individual errors, and that broad categories, such as 'bumblebee' or 'solitary bee', are sufficient to detect long-term trends."}, {"l": "G", "t": "The study of urban bees has also produced findings of wider scientific interest. Researchers in Montreal recently discovered that bees collected in the city centre carried pollen from a much greater variety of plants than those collected on the outskirts, including many ornamental species originating from other continents. This suggests that some bee species are more flexible in their diet than had previously been assumed. Whether this flexibility represents a genuine adaptation to urban life, or simply reflects the range of plants available, is a question that will require experiments over several generations to resolve."}, {"l": "H", "t": "Ultimately, the presence of thriving bee communities in cities should not be taken as evidence that urbanisation is harmless. Rather, it highlights how severely the countryside has been degraded. Many ecologists now argue that cities can serve as refuges from which pollinators might eventually recolonise agricultural land, provided that farming practices change. If that is to happen, the lessons learnt from urban gardens, such as the value of continuous flowering and undisturbed nesting sites, will need to be applied on a far larger scale."}], "groups": [{"type": "headings", "instr": "Questions 1–5. Choose the correct heading for paragraphs B–F from the list below. There are more headings than paragraphs.", "opts": ["i  Why farmland has become less suitable for bees", "ii  Measures taken by local governments", "iii  The economic value of pollination", "iv  Not all species benefit equally", "v  Relying on non-specialists to gather information", "vi  A popular hobby with unintended consequences", "vii  The decline of honey production", "viii  A surprising discovery about bee diets"], "items": [{"q": "Paragraph B", "a": "i", "loc": "B", "s": "The explanation for this apparent paradox lies largely in the transformation of modern agriculture.", "ex": "Абзац B объясняет, почему сельхозугодья стали непригодны: монокультуры, удаление живых изгородей, гербициды.", "trap": "Заголовок iii (экономическая ценность) — ловушка: речь о сельском хозяйстве, но не об экономике."}, {"q": "Paragraph C", "a": "iv", "loc": "C", "s": "the benefits are unevenly distributed", "ex": "«Benefits are unevenly distributed» = не все виды выигрывают одинаково (наземные пчёлы страдают).", "trap": "Цифра «70%» отвлекает внимание, но главная идея — неравенство видов."}, {"q": "Paragraph D", "a": "vi", "loc": "D", "s": "Ecologists have expressed concern that this enthusiasm may be counterproductive.", "ex": "Городское пчеловодство (хобби) может навредить диким пчёлам — непредвиденные последствия.", "trap": "vii (упадок производства мёда) — мёд не упоминается; слово honeybee провоцирует ошибку."}, {"q": "Paragraph E", "a": "ii", "loc": "E", "s": "several municipal authorities have begun to adopt policies", "ex": "Municipal authorities/councils = local governments.", "trap": "Последнее предложение о сложности измерения — второстепенная деталь, а не тема абзаца."}, {"q": "Paragraph F", "a": "v", "loc": "F", "s": "a number of projects now rely on volunteers to collect data", "ex": "Volunteers / members of the public = non-specialists; collect data = gather information.", "trap": "Критика (untrained observers) — часть абзаца, но заголовок описывает его целиком."}]}, {"type": "tfng", "instr": "Questions 6–9. Do the following statements agree with the information in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE or NOT GIVEN.", "items": [{"q": "Some cities have been found to contain a wider range of bee species than nearby rural areas.", "a": "TRUE", "loc": "A", "s": "appear to be more diverse than those found in the surrounding countryside", "ex": "More diverse than countryside = wider range of species. Перефразирование.", "trap": "Слово «in some cases» — не делает утверждение ложным, т.к. в вопросе тоже «some cities»."}, {"q": "Most bee species build their nests underground.", "a": "TRUE", "loc": "C", "s": "ground-nesting bees, which make up roughly seventy per cent of all species", "ex": "70% = большинство (most).", "trap": "Ответ спрятан в цитате эксперта — легко пропустить."}, {"q": "The number of hives in London fell after 2018.", "a": "NOT GIVEN", "loc": "D", "s": "more than doubled between 2008 and 2018", "ex": "Текст говорит только о периоде 2008–2018; о том, что было после, информации нет.", "trap": "Классическая ловушка NG: упомянут год 2018, но данных после него нет — это не FALSE."}, {"q": "It has been proven that honeybees spread diseases to wild bees.", "a": "FALSE", "loc": "D", "s": "although the extent of this risk remains disputed", "ex": "Риск «оспаривается» (disputed) — значит, не доказан (proven). Противоречие.", "trap": "Слово «proven» сильнее, чем «warned» в тексте — следите за степенью уверенности."}]}, {"type": "complete", "instr": "Questions 10–13. Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.", "limit": 2, "items": [{"q": "In Utrecht, the roofs of bus shelters have been turned into small ______.", "a": ["meadows", "miniature meadows"], "loc": "E", "s": "into miniature meadows planted with sedum", "ex": "«converted … into miniature meadows» — ответ meadows (или miniature meadows, 2 слова).", "trap": "Sedum — название растения, а не то, во что превращены крыши."}, {"q": "Changes in ______ make it hard to judge whether pollinator policies work.", "a": ["weather conditions", "weather"], "loc": "E", "s": "fluctuate considerably from year to year in response to weather conditions", "ex": "Колебания популяций из-за погоды мешают оценить эффективность мер.", "trap": "«year to year» — не подходит по смыслу."}, {"q": "Many bee species can only be identified using a ______.", "a": ["microscope"], "loc": "F", "s": "can only be identified under a microscope", "ex": "Прямое указание в тексте.", "trap": "—"}, {"q": "Bees in central Montreal carried pollen from many ______ plants from other continents.", "a": ["ornamental"], "loc": "G", "s": "including many ornamental species originating from other continents", "ex": "Ornamental species — декоративные растения.", "trap": "«variety» стоит рядом, но не подходит грамматически."}]}]}, {"title": "The Value of Forgetting", "topic": "Psychology", "paras": [{"l": "A", "t": "Forgetting has long been viewed as a failure of the mind, a regrettable leak in an otherwise impressive system. Students lament the formulas that vanish the night before an examination, and older adults often interpret a forgotten name as an early sign of decline. In popular culture, too, the ideal memory is a perfect one: the fictional detective who recalls every detail of a crime scene is admired precisely because he forgets nothing. Over the last thirty years, however, psychologists and neuroscientists have begun to argue that forgetting is not merely a defect but an active and useful process, without which human thought would be considerably less efficient."}, {"l": "B", "t": "Some of the most striking evidence comes from rare individuals who appear unable to forget. In the 1920s, the Russian psychologist Alexander Luria began studying a journalist, known in the literature as S., who could reproduce long lists of numbers or nonsense syllables years after hearing them only once. Far from benefiting from this ability, S. found everyday life confusing. Because each experience was stored in vivid and specific detail, he struggled to recognise faces that had changed expression, and he found it difficult to grasp abstract ideas or metaphors, since particular images constantly intruded on his thoughts. His case suggests that the capacity to discard detail may be essential for forming general concepts."}, {"l": "C", "t": "This idea has been supported by laboratory studies of what researchers call 'retrieval-induced forgetting'. In a typical experiment, participants learn several words in each of a number of categories, for example fruits and tools, and then practise recalling only some of the fruit words. When they are later tested on everything they learnt, they remember the unpractised fruit words less well than the unpractised tools. The act of retrieving certain items, it seems, actively suppresses competing items in the same category. Although this may appear to be a drawback, many psychologists interpret it as a mechanism that reduces interference, making it easier to locate the information we use most often."}, {"l": "D", "t": "Neuroscience has begun to identify the biological basis of such processes. For many years, it was assumed that memories faded simply because the connections between neurons weakened over time through lack of use. More recent work with laboratory animals indicates that the brain also possesses dedicated systems for erasing memories. In experiments on fruit flies, for instance, researchers have identified a chemical signalling pathway that gradually degrades newly formed memories; when this pathway is blocked, the flies retain what they have learnt for much longer. Similar mechanisms are thought to exist in mammals, including humans, although they are far harder to study directly."}, {"l": "E", "t": "Sleep appears to play a crucial role in deciding which memories survive. According to one influential hypothesis, the connections formed between neurons during the day are scaled back during deep sleep, so that only the strongest are preserved. This pruning, it is argued, prevents the brain from becoming saturated and conserves energy, since maintaining connections is metabolically expensive. Not all researchers accept this account, and some point to studies showing that sleep can strengthen even weak memories if they are linked to a future reward. Nevertheless, there is broad agreement that sleep does not simply preserve experience but reorganises it."}, {"l": "F", "t": "The practical implications of this research are considerable. In education, the finding that partial forgetting can enhance later learning has led to renewed interest in 'spaced practice', in which material is revisited after intervals long enough for some of it to have faded. Students who allow themselves to forget a little, and then make the effort to retrieve the information, tend to retain it far longer than those who repeat it continuously in a single session. The struggle to remember, rather than being a sign that learning has failed, appears to be what makes it durable."}, {"l": "G", "t": "Clinicians, meanwhile, are exploring whether an understanding of forgetting could help people who are troubled by memories they cannot escape. Individuals suffering from post-traumatic stress disorder often experience intrusive recollections that are triggered by everyday sights and sounds. Some experimental therapies attempt to exploit the fact that a memory becomes temporarily unstable each time it is recalled, offering a window during which its emotional intensity might be reduced. Early results are encouraging, but researchers stress that such treatments are intended to weaken the distress associated with a memory, not to erase the memory itself, a distinction that raises important ethical questions."}, {"l": "H", "t": "None of this means that all forgetting is beneficial. Diseases such as Alzheimer's cause a devastating and indiscriminate loss of memory that bears no resemblance to the selective processes described above. The emerging view is rather that a healthy memory is defined not by how much it stores, but by how well it balances retention and loss. As one researcher has put it, the purpose of memory is not to record the past but to guide future behaviour, and for that purpose, a degree of forgetting is not a weakness but a necessity."}], "groups": [{"type": "matchinfo", "instr": "Questions 14–18. Which paragraph contains the following information? Write the correct letter, A–H.", "opts": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E", "F", "G", "H"], "items": [{"q": "a description of a method of studying that makes use of forgetting", "a": "F", "loc": "F", "s": "renewed interest in 'spaced practice'", "ex": "Spaced practice — метод обучения, использующий частичное забывание.", "trap": "Абзац C тоже про эксперименты с запоминанием, но не про метод обучения."}, {"q": "an example of a person whose excellent memory caused difficulties", "a": "B", "loc": "B", "s": "Far from benefiting from this ability, S. found everyday life confusing.", "ex": "Журналист S. — пример человека, которому мешала идеальная память.", "trap": "Абзац A упоминает вымышленного детектива с идеальной памятью, но без трудностей."}, {"q": "a reference to the energy cost of keeping memories", "a": "E", "loc": "E", "s": "maintaining connections is metabolically expensive", "ex": "Metabolically expensive = energy cost.", "trap": "Синоним спрятан: в вопросе «energy», в тексте «conserves energy / metabolically»."}, {"q": "a warning about the moral issues raised by a type of treatment", "a": "G", "loc": "G", "s": "a distinction that raises important ethical questions", "ex": "Ethical questions = moral issues.", "trap": "Слово «encouraging» может сбить: позитив, но в конце — предупреждение."}, {"q": "evidence that the brain has specific systems for removing memories", "a": "D", "loc": "D", "s": "the brain also possesses dedicated systems for erasing memories", "ex": "Dedicated systems for erasing = specific systems for removing.", "trap": "Абзац E (сон) тоже про «удаление» связей, но не про специальные системы."}]}, {"type": "ynng", "instr": "Questions 19–22. Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer? Write YES, NO or NOT GIVEN.", "items": [{"q": "Popular culture tends to present perfect memory as desirable.", "a": "YES", "loc": "A", "s": "In popular culture, too, the ideal memory is a perfect one", "ex": "Автор прямо говорит, что в поп-культуре идеальная память — идеал.", "trap": "—"}, {"q": "S. was better than most people at understanding metaphors.", "a": "NO", "loc": "B", "s": "he found it difficult to grasp abstract ideas or metaphors", "ex": "Ему было трудно понимать метафоры — противоположность утверждению.", "trap": "Его превосходная память может навести на мысль, что он лучше во всём."}, {"q": "Retrieval-induced forgetting is more common in older adults.", "a": "NOT GIVEN", "loc": "C", "s": "", "ex": "Возраст участников экспериментов не упоминается.", "trap": "Пожилые люди упомянуты в абзаце A — но не в связи с этим эффектом."}, {"q": "Therapies for PTSD aim to remove traumatic memories completely.", "a": "NO", "loc": "G", "s": "not to erase the memory itself", "ex": "Цель — ослабить переживания, а не стереть память.", "trap": "Слово «exploit … unstable» звучит радикально, но автор опровергает."}]}, {"type": "mcq", "instr": "Questions 23–24. Choose the correct letter, A, B, C or D.", "items": [{"q": "In the experiment described in paragraph C, the unpractised fruit words were", "opts": ["A remembered better than the tools.", "B remembered less well than the unpractised tools.", "C forgotten completely.", "D practised more than the tools."], "a": "B", "loc": "C", "s": "they remember the unpractised fruit words less well than the unpractised tools", "ex": "Прямо сказано: хуже, чем неотработанные инструменты.", "trap": "C — слишком сильное утверждение (completely)."}, {"q": "What is the writer's main conclusion?", "opts": ["A Forgetting is always a sign of illness.", "B Memory works best when it stores everything.", "C A good memory balances keeping and losing information.", "D Sleep is the only cause of forgetting."], "a": "C", "loc": "H", "s": "how well it balances retention and loss", "ex": "Здоровая память — баланс сохранения и потери.", "trap": "D — сон упомянут, но «only» делает вариант неверным."}]}, {"type": "complete", "instr": "Questions 25–27. Complete the summary. Choose ONE WORD ONLY from the passage for each answer.", "limit": 1, "items": [{"q": "During deep sleep, connections between neurons may be scaled back, a process described as ______.", "a": ["pruning"], "loc": "E", "s": "This pruning, it is argued", "ex": "Pruning — «обрезка» связей.", "trap": "«saturated» — описывает проблему, а не процесс."}, {"q": "Some studies show sleep can strengthen weak memories that are connected to a future ______.", "a": ["reward"], "loc": "E", "s": "if they are linked to a future reward", "ex": "Linked to = connected to.", "trap": "—"}, {"q": "Each time a memory is recalled, it becomes temporarily ______.", "a": ["unstable"], "loc": "G", "s": "a memory becomes temporarily unstable each time it is recalled", "ex": "Прямой ответ.", "trap": "«intrusive» — характеристика воспоминаний при ПТСР, не подходит."}]}]}, {"title": "Salt: The Mineral That Shaped Civilisations", "topic": "History", "paras": [{"l": "A", "t": "Today salt is so cheap and abundant that it is difficult to imagine it was once among the most valuable commodities on earth. Yet for most of human history, the ability to obtain salt determined where people settled, which routes merchants travelled and, on occasion, which empires prospered. Humans need a small quantity of sodium to survive, but the demand for salt rose dramatically once agricultural societies began to rely on it for a far more important purpose: preserving food. Before refrigeration, meat, fish and vegetables could be kept through the winter only if they were dried, smoked or, most reliably, salted."}, {"l": "B", "t": "The earliest known evidence of large-scale salt production comes from a site in northern China, where archaeologists have found the remains of facilities dating back more than four thousand years. There, workers boiled brine from a salt lake in clay vessels until the water evaporated. In Europe, the Celtic communities of what is now Austria mined rock salt from deep underground, and the wealth generated by the trade allowed them to acquire luxury goods from as far away as the Mediterranean. The town of Hallstatt, which gave its name to an entire period of European prehistory, owes its existence almost entirely to these mines."}, {"l": "C", "t": "Because salt was essential and could be produced in only a limited number of places, rulers quickly recognised that controlling it offered a reliable source of revenue. In China, the state established a monopoly on salt as early as the seventh century BCE, and for much of the following two thousand years salt taxes provided a substantial proportion of government income. A famous debate held at the imperial court in 81 BCE, recorded in a text known as the Discourses on Salt and Iron, pitted officials who defended the monopoly against scholars who argued that it placed an unfair burden on ordinary people."}, {"l": "D", "t": "The Romans, too, understood the strategic value of salt. One of the oldest roads in Italy, the Via Salaria, was built to transport salt from coastal marshes near the mouth of the Tiber to settlements in the interior. It is often claimed that Roman soldiers were paid in salt, and that the English word 'salary' derives from this practice. Historians now treat this story with caution: although the word does come from the Latin for salt, there is little direct evidence that soldiers received salt in place of money, and the term may instead have referred to an allowance for buying it."}, {"l": "E", "t": "In West Africa, salt became the basis of one of the most extensive trading systems of the medieval world. Caravans of camels carried slabs of salt southwards across the Sahara from mines such as Taghaza, where buildings were reportedly constructed from blocks of the mineral itself. In the markets of the Sahel, salt was exchanged for gold, ivory and other goods from the forest regions further south. Travellers' accounts suggest that in some places salt was so scarce that it was traded weight for weight with gold, although modern scholars believe such reports were probably exaggerated."}, {"l": "F", "t": "The importance of salt meant that attempts to tax it could provoke serious resistance. In France, the gabelle, a salt tax first introduced in the fourteenth century, became one of the most hated features of the old regime. The tax varied enormously from one province to another, encouraging widespread smuggling, and thousands of people were imprisoned each year for trading salt illegally. Resentment of the gabelle is widely regarded as one of the grievances that contributed to the French Revolution, and it was abolished in 1790, only to be reintroduced by Napoleon some years later."}, {"l": "G", "t": "Perhaps the most famous protest against a salt tax took place in British-ruled India. In 1930, Mohandas Gandhi led a march of nearly four hundred kilometres to the coastal village of Dandi, where he picked up a handful of natural salt in defiance of laws that gave the colonial government a monopoly on its production. The act was symbolic, but its consequences were profound. Within weeks, tens of thousands of people across India had followed his example, and the campaign drew international attention to the movement for independence."}, {"l": "H", "t": "The decline of salt as a strategic resource began in the nineteenth century, when geologists discovered vast underground deposits and new techniques made extraction far cheaper. The invention of canning and, later, refrigeration reduced the need to preserve food with salt. Today, the greatest concern among health experts is not that people consume too little salt but that they consume too much, since excessive intake is associated with high blood pressure. The mineral that once financed empires is now more often the subject of public health campaigns."}], "groups": [{"type": "headings", "instr": "Questions 28–31. Choose the correct heading for paragraphs C, D, F and H from the list below.", "opts": ["i  A source of income for governments", "ii  The first salt mines in Africa", "iii  Questioning a well-known story", "iv  A tax that led to unrest", "v  From precious resource to health risk", "vi  Salt in religious ceremonies", "vii  The invention of refrigeration"], "items": [{"q": "Paragraph C", "a": "i", "loc": "C", "s": "controlling it offered a reliable source of revenue", "ex": "Revenue / government income = source of income for governments.", "trap": "Дебаты 81 г. до н.э. — деталь, а не главная мысль."}, {"q": "Paragraph D", "a": "iii", "loc": "D", "s": "Historians now treat this story with caution", "ex": "История о жалованье солью ставится под сомнение.", "trap": "Via Salaria упоминается первой, но это не главная идея абзаца."}, {"q": "Paragraph F", "a": "iv", "loc": "F", "s": "attempts to tax it could provoke serious resistance", "ex": "Gabelle вызвал сопротивление и стал одной из причин революции.", "trap": "Абзац G тоже о протесте, но заголовок iv точнее подходит к F (налог → беспорядки)."}, {"q": "Paragraph H", "a": "v", "loc": "H", "s": "The mineral that once financed empires is now more often the subject of public health campaigns.", "ex": "От ценного ресурса — к угрозе здоровью.", "trap": "vii — холодильники упомянуты, но это лишь одна деталь."}]}, {"type": "tfng", "instr": "Questions 32–36. Do the following statements agree with the information in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE or NOT GIVEN.", "items": [{"q": "Humans began to need more salt after they started farming.", "a": "TRUE", "loc": "A", "s": "the demand for salt rose dramatically once agricultural societies began to rely on it", "ex": "Спрос резко вырос с развитием сельского хозяйства.", "trap": "Речь о спросе общества, а не о биологической потребности — но утверждение всё равно TRUE по смыслу текста."}, {"q": "The salt facilities in northern China used rock salt mined underground.", "a": "FALSE", "loc": "B", "s": "workers boiled brine from a salt lake", "ex": "В Китае выпаривали рассол из озера; добыча из шахт — в Австрии.", "trap": "Смешение двух примеров в одном абзаце."}, {"q": "There is strong evidence that Roman soldiers were paid in salt.", "a": "FALSE", "loc": "D", "s": "there is little direct evidence that soldiers received salt", "ex": "Little evidence ≠ strong evidence.", "trap": "Этимология слова salary верна, но это не доказательство оплаты солью."}, {"q": "Buildings in Taghaza were made of salt.", "a": "TRUE", "loc": "E", "s": "buildings were reportedly constructed from blocks of the mineral itself", "ex": "Reportedly — по сообщениям; утверждение совпадает с текстом.", "trap": "Слово reportedly не делает ответ NG — текст сообщает это как факт из источников."}, {"q": "Napoleon reduced the rate of the gabelle when he reintroduced it.", "a": "NOT GIVEN", "loc": "F", "s": "only to be reintroduced by Napoleon some years later", "ex": "О ставке налога при Наполеоне ничего не сказано.", "trap": "Упоминание Наполеона провоцирует ответ, но данных о ставке нет."}]}, {"type": "table", "instr": "Questions 37–40. Complete the table. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.", "limit": 2, "head": ["Place", "Key fact"], "rows": [["Hallstatt", "town existed because of salt ______ (37)"], ["China, 81 BCE", "debate recorded in the Discourses on Salt and ______ (38)"], ["Sahel markets", "salt exchanged for gold, ______ (39) and other goods"], ["Dandi, 1930", "Gandhi's march of nearly 400 ______ (40)"]], "items": [{"q": "37", "a": ["mines"], "loc": "B", "s": "owes its existence almost entirely to these mines", "ex": "Город существует благодаря соляным шахтам.", "trap": "«trade» тоже рядом, но текст говорит именно «mines»."}, {"q": "38", "a": ["iron"], "loc": "C", "s": "Discourses on Salt and Iron", "ex": "Название текста.", "trap": "—"}, {"q": "39", "a": ["ivory"], "loc": "E", "s": "exchanged for gold, ivory and other goods", "ex": "Прямой ответ.", "trap": "«slabs» — форма соли, а не товар обмена."}, {"q": "40", "a": ["kilometres", "kilometers"], "loc": "G", "s": "a march of nearly four hundred kilometres", "ex": "Единица измерения расстояния.", "trap": "Нельзя писать «miles» — используйте слово из текста."}]}]}, {"title": "Growing Meat Without Animals", "topic": "Technology / Food science", "paras": [{"l": "A", "t": "In 2013, a London audience watched a chef fry a burger that had cost more than a quarter of a million dollars to produce. The patty contained no meat from a slaughtered animal; instead, it had been assembled from tens of billions of muscle cells grown in a laboratory from a small sample of tissue taken from a living cow. The tasters were polite but unenthusiastic, describing the texture as close to meat but lacking in flavour, largely because the patty contained no fat. Nevertheless, the event marked the beginning of an industry that now attracts billions of dollars in investment and promises, according to its supporters, to transform the way humanity feeds itself."}, {"l": "B", "t": "The case for cultivated meat, as it is now usually called, rests on the environmental and ethical costs of conventional livestock farming. Animal agriculture occupies roughly three-quarters of the world's farmland while supplying less than a fifth of its calories. It is responsible for a significant share of global greenhouse gas emissions, notably methane released by cattle, and is a leading cause of deforestation in regions such as the Amazon. Advocates argue that producing meat directly from cells could dramatically reduce the land and water required, as well as eliminating the slaughter of animals."}, {"l": "C", "t": "The production process begins with the collection of a small number of cells, usually by means of a painless biopsy. Scientists select cells capable of multiplying rapidly, such as stem cells or precursor muscle cells, and place them in a nutrient-rich liquid known as the growth medium. The cells are then transferred to large steel vessels called bioreactors, which resemble those used in brewing, where temperature and oxygen levels are carefully controlled. Once the cells have multiplied sufficiently, they are encouraged to differentiate into muscle and fat, and are finally harvested and formed into products."}, {"l": "D", "t": "Each stage presents formidable technical challenges. The growth medium has traditionally relied on foetal bovine serum, a substance extracted from the blood of unborn calves, which is both expensive and difficult to reconcile with the industry's ethical claims. Several companies have now developed serum-free alternatives, but these still account for the majority of production costs. Scaling up is equally problematic: cells grown in very large bioreactors are vulnerable to contamination by bacteria, and a single infected batch must be discarded entirely. For this reason, some engineers believe that a network of smaller facilities may prove more practical than a few enormous plants."}, {"l": "E", "t": "Producing a recognisable steak or chicken breast is harder still. Muscle tissue in a living animal is supported by a network of blood vessels that supply nutrients and remove waste. Without such a network, cells in the centre of a thick piece of tissue are starved of oxygen and die. Researchers are therefore experimenting with edible scaffolds, made from plant proteins or fungal fibres, around which cells can grow in three dimensions. Until these methods mature, most cultivated products are likely to be minced or processed items such as burgers, sausages and nuggets."}, {"l": "F", "t": "The environmental benefits, too, are less certain than early enthusiasts suggested. A widely cited analysis published by researchers at the University of California concluded that, if bioreactors are powered by electricity generated from fossil fuels, and if the growth medium must be highly purified, the carbon footprint of cultivated meat could actually exceed that of beef. Other studies have reached more optimistic conclusions, but almost all agree that the outcome depends heavily on the use of renewable energy. In other words, cultivated meat is unlikely to be greener than conventional meat unless the energy system itself becomes greener."}, {"l": "G", "t": "Regulators have approached the new products cautiously. Singapore became the first country to approve the sale of cultivated chicken in 2020, followed by the United States in 2023. Elsewhere, the reaction has been more hostile. Several jurisdictions have proposed or passed laws banning the sale of cultivated meat, citing the need to protect traditional farmers and culinary heritage, while others have restricted the use of words such as 'meat' on labels. Surveys of consumers reveal a similar ambivalence: many people say they would be willing to try the products, but far fewer say they would eat them regularly."}, {"l": "H", "t": "Whether cultivated meat becomes a mainstream food or remains a niche product will probably depend less on technology than on price. Industry estimates suggest that costs have fallen by more than ninety-nine per cent since 2013, yet a cultivated burger remains considerably more expensive than its conventional equivalent. Some analysts predict that hybrid products, which combine a small proportion of animal cells with plant-based ingredients, will reach supermarkets first. If so, the first experience most consumers have of meat grown without animals may be one they barely notice."}], "groups": [{"type": "matchinfo", "instr": "Questions 1–4. Which paragraph contains the following information? Write the correct letter, A–H.", "opts": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E", "F", "G", "H"], "items": [{"q": "legal measures intended to prevent cultivated meat from being sold", "a": "G", "loc": "G", "s": "laws banning the sale of cultivated meat", "ex": "Laws banning the sale = legal measures to prevent selling.", "trap": "Одобрение в Сингапуре и США — тоже регулирование, но противоположное."}, {"q": "a comparison between land use and food energy produced by livestock", "a": "B", "loc": "B", "s": "occupies roughly three-quarters of the world's farmland while supplying less than a fifth of its calories", "ex": "Land (farmland) vs. food energy (calories).", "trap": "Калории = food energy — перефразирование."}, {"q": "the reason why most products will initially be processed foods", "a": "E", "loc": "E", "s": "most cultivated products are likely to be minced or processed items", "ex": "Нет сосудистой сети → пока только фарш/наггетсы.", "trap": "Абзац H про гибридные продукты — другая причина."}, {"q": "a description of the first public tasting", "a": "A", "loc": "A", "s": "a London audience watched a chef fry a burger", "ex": "Дегустация 2013 г.", "trap": "—"}]}, {"type": "flow", "instr": "Questions 5–9. Complete the flow-chart. Choose ONE WORD ONLY from the passage for each answer.", "limit": 1, "steps": ["Cells collected by a painless ______ (5)", "↓", "Cells placed in a nutrient-rich ______ (6) medium", "↓", "Cells grow in steel vessels called ______ (7)", "↓", "Cells encouraged to ______ (8) into muscle and fat", "↓", "Cells ______ (9) and formed into products"], "items": [{"q": "5", "a": ["biopsy"], "loc": "C", "s": "usually by means of a painless biopsy", "ex": "Прямой ответ.", "trap": "—"}, {"q": "6", "a": ["growth"], "loc": "C", "s": "a nutrient-rich liquid known as the growth medium", "ex": "Growth medium.", "trap": "«liquid» не подходит: слово перед medium — growth."}, {"q": "7", "a": ["bioreactors"], "loc": "C", "s": "large steel vessels called bioreactors", "ex": "Прямой ответ.", "trap": "«brewing» — сравнение, а не название."}, {"q": "8", "a": ["differentiate"], "loc": "C", "s": "they are encouraged to differentiate into muscle and fat", "ex": "Differentiate — превращаться в специализированные клетки.", "trap": "«multiply» — предыдущий этап."}, {"q": "9", "a": ["harvested"], "loc": "C", "s": "are finally harvested and formed into products", "ex": "Прямой ответ.", "trap": "—"}]}, {"type": "tfng", "instr": "Questions 10–13. Write TRUE, FALSE or NOT GIVEN.", "items": [{"q": "Foetal bovine serum is cheap but ethically controversial.", "a": "FALSE", "loc": "D", "s": "which is both expensive and difficult to reconcile with the industry's ethical claims", "ex": "Сыворотка дорогая, а не дешёвая.", "trap": "Вторая часть верна, но одна ошибка делает всё утверждение FALSE."}, {"q": "All studies agree that cultivated meat has a larger carbon footprint than beef.", "a": "FALSE", "loc": "F", "s": "Other studies have reached more optimistic conclusions", "ex": "Есть исследования с более оптимистичными выводами.", "trap": "«All» — сигнальное слово крайности."}, {"q": "Most consumers surveyed would eat cultivated meat every day.", "a": "FALSE", "loc": "G", "s": "far fewer say they would eat them regularly", "ex": "Регулярно — гораздо меньше людей.", "trap": "«willing to try» ≠ eat regularly."}, {"q": "Hybrid products are already sold in most European supermarkets.", "a": "NOT GIVEN", "loc": "H", "s": "will reach supermarkets first", "ex": "Говорится только о прогнозе; о Европе нет данных.", "trap": "Будущее время в тексте vs настоящее в утверждении — но о текущих продажах в Европе информации нет."}]}]}, {"title": "Mining the Deep Ocean", "topic": "Environment / Technology", "paras": [{"l": "A", "t": "Several kilometres beneath the surface of the Pacific Ocean, scattered across a vast plain of mud between Hawaii and Mexico, lie trillions of dark, potato-sized lumps known as polymetallic nodules. Formed over millions of years as metals dissolved in seawater accumulated around fragments of shell or rock, the nodules are rich in manganese, nickel, cobalt and copper, all of which are essential components of the batteries that power electric vehicles. To mining companies, this region, known as the Clarion-Clipperton Zone, represents one of the largest untapped mineral resources on the planet. To many marine biologists, it represents one of the least understood ecosystems on earth."}, {"l": "B", "t": "The idea of harvesting minerals from the seabed is not new. In the 1970s, several consortia tested prototype collectors in the Pacific, and some commentators predicted that deep-sea mining would become a major industry by the end of the century. Those predictions proved premature. Metal prices fell, the technical difficulties turned out to be greater than expected, and the legal framework governing the international seabed remained uncertain. Interest has revived only recently, driven by the rapidly growing demand for battery metals and by concerns that land-based supplies are concentrated in a small number of countries."}, {"l": "C", "t": "Under the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea, the seabed beyond national jurisdiction is designated as the 'common heritage of mankind'. Its mineral resources are managed by the International Seabed Authority, a body based in Jamaica that has granted more than thirty exploration contracts but has not yet approved commercial extraction. The Authority faces a difficult task: it is required both to encourage the development of seabed resources and to ensure the effective protection of the marine environment. Critics argue that these two responsibilities are fundamentally in conflict, and that a single organisation cannot credibly fulfil both."}, {"l": "D", "t": "Proponents of seabed mining present it as the lesser of two evils. Mining on land frequently involves clearing forests, displacing communities and producing enormous quantities of toxic waste. In some countries, cobalt is extracted under dangerous conditions, sometimes by children. Nodules, by contrast, lie loose on the seabed and can in principle be collected without blasting or digging, and they contain several valuable metals in a single deposit. Companies also point out that no people live on the abyssal plains, so there is no risk of displacing human populations."}, {"l": "E", "t": "Scientists, however, warn that the deep sea is far from empty. Expeditions to the Clarion-Clipperton Zone have found that a large majority of the animals collected there are new to science, including sponges, worms, sea cucumbers and translucent octopuses that lay their eggs on the stalks of dead sponges attached to nodules. Because the nodules themselves provide the only hard surface in an expanse of soft sediment, removing them would eliminate the habitat of many species. Given that the nodules grow at a rate of only a few millimetres per million years, this loss would be, by any human measure, permanent."}, {"l": "F", "t": "The effects of mining may also extend far beyond the areas directly harvested. Collector vehicles moving across the seabed would stir up plumes of sediment that could drift for tens or even hundreds of kilometres, smothering filter-feeding organisms. Noise and light from mining operations could disturb animals adapted to darkness and silence. In 1989, German researchers ploughed a section of seabed off the coast of Peru to simulate the impact of mining; when they returned nearly three decades later, the tracks were still clearly visible and many animal populations had not recovered."}, {"l": "G", "t": "Faced with such uncertainty, a growing number of governments have called for a pause, or moratorium, on deep-sea mining until its consequences are better understood. Several major car manufacturers and technology companies have pledged not to use metals from the seabed in their products. At the same time, advances in battery chemistry are reducing the industry's dependence on some metals: batteries based on lithium iron phosphate, for example, contain no cobalt or nickel at all. If such technologies continue to spread, the economic argument for mining the ocean floor may weaken considerably."}, {"l": "H", "t": "Nevertheless, the pressure to begin extraction remains strong. Some countries argue that the energy transition cannot be achieved without new sources of minerals, and that delaying seabed mining merely shifts environmental damage onto land. The debate thus raises a question that is likely to recur as societies attempt to reduce their carbon emissions: how should the urgent need to combat climate change be weighed against the protection of ecosystems that most people will never see?"}], "groups": [{"type": "headings", "instr": "Questions 14–18. Choose the correct heading for paragraphs B–F.", "opts": ["i  An organisation with conflicting duties", "ii  Long-lasting damage from an earlier experiment", "iii  A failed early attempt and a recent revival", "iv  Arguments in favour of extraction", "v  Habitats that cannot be replaced", "vi  The high cost of underwater robots", "vii  The formation of nodules"], "items": [{"q": "Paragraph B", "a": "iii", "loc": "B", "s": "Those predictions proved premature.", "ex": "Попытки 1970-х не удались, интерес вернулся недавно.", "trap": "vii — формирование конкреций описано в A, не в B."}, {"q": "Paragraph C", "a": "i", "loc": "C", "s": "these two responsibilities are fundamentally in conflict", "ex": "ISA должна и развивать добычу, и защищать среду.", "trap": "Детали о Ямайке и контрактах отвлекают."}, {"q": "Paragraph D", "a": "iv", "loc": "D", "s": "Proponents of seabed mining present it as the lesser of two evils.", "ex": "Proponents = сторонники, аргументы за.", "trap": "Описание вреда добычи на суше — часть аргумента «за» морскую добычу."}, {"q": "Paragraph E", "a": "v", "loc": "E", "s": "this loss would be, by any human measure, permanent", "ex": "Конкреции — единственная твёрдая поверхность, восстановление невозможно.", "trap": "Новые виды — деталь; главная идея — незаменимые места обитания."}, {"q": "Paragraph F", "a": "ii", "loc": "F", "s": "the tracks were still clearly visible", "ex": "Эксперимент 1989 г.: следы видны спустя десятилетия.", "trap": "Шум и свет — тоже последствия, но заголовок ii точнее всего."}]}, {"type": "ynng", "instr": "Questions 19–22. Do the statements agree with the views of the writer? Write YES, NO or NOT GIVEN.", "items": [{"q": "Predictions made in the 1970s about deep-sea mining were accurate.", "a": "NO", "loc": "B", "s": "Those predictions proved premature.", "ex": "Premature = преждевременные, не сбылись.", "trap": "—"}, {"q": "Collecting nodules requires blasting the seabed.", "a": "NO", "loc": "D", "s": "can in principle be collected without blasting or digging", "ex": "Без взрывов и копания.", "trap": "Слово «in principle» не меняет смысла."}, {"q": "The animals of the Clarion-Clipperton Zone are more colourful than shallow-water species.", "a": "NOT GIVEN", "loc": "E", "s": "", "ex": "Цвет животных не сравнивается (упомянуты лишь прозрачные осьминоги).", "trap": "«translucent» может натолкнуть на мысль о цвете."}, {"q": "Changes in battery technology could reduce the need for seabed metals.", "a": "YES", "loc": "G", "s": "advances in battery chemistry are reducing the industry's dependence on some metals", "ex": "LFP-батареи без кобальта и никеля.", "trap": "—"}]}, {"type": "complete", "instr": "Questions 23–26. Complete the summary. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage.", "limit": 2, "items": [{"q": "Nodules contain metals needed for the ______ used in electric vehicles.", "a": ["batteries"], "loc": "A", "s": "essential components of the batteries", "ex": "Прямой ответ.", "trap": "«vehicles» уже в предложении."}, {"q": "The seabed beyond national control is called the common ______ of mankind.", "a": ["heritage"], "loc": "C", "s": "'common heritage of mankind'", "ex": "Прямой ответ.", "trap": "—"}, {"q": "Mining vehicles could create ______ of sediment that travel long distances.", "a": ["plumes"], "loc": "F", "s": "stir up plumes of sediment", "ex": "Plumes — облака/шлейфы.", "trap": "«tracks» — следы на дне, не облака."}, {"q": "Many governments want a ______ on mining until more is known.", "a": ["moratorium", "pause"], "loc": "G", "s": "a pause, or moratorium", "ex": "Оба слова допустимы.", "trap": "—"}]}]}, {"title": "The Paradox of Choice", "topic": "Psychology / Economics", "paras": [{"l": "A", "t": "It is widely assumed in modern economies that more choice is always better. If a shop offers fifty varieties of jam rather than five, the reasoning goes, every customer is more likely to find exactly what he or she wants, and those who are indifferent can simply ignore the extra options. This belief underpins much of contemporary consumer culture, from the endless aisles of supermarkets to the thousands of programmes available on streaming platforms. Since the late 1990s, however, a series of studies in psychology and behavioural economics has suggested that, beyond a certain point, an increase in options can make people less likely to choose at all, and less satisfied when they do."}, {"l": "B", "t": "The most frequently cited study was conducted in a Californian supermarket by the psychologists Sheena Iyengar and Mark Lepper. On some days, shoppers passing a tasting stand were offered a selection of twenty-four jams; on others, only six. The larger display attracted more visitors, but when it came to buying, the result was striking: around thirty per cent of those who had seen the small selection made a purchase, compared with just three per cent of those who had seen the large one. The researchers concluded that an abundance of options, while initially appealing, could discourage people from making a decision."}, {"l": "C", "t": "Subsequent research appeared to confirm the effect in a wide range of settings. Employees offered a larger number of investment funds in their pension schemes were found to be less likely to join the scheme at all. Students given a long list of essay topics wrote essays of lower quality than those given a short list. The psychologist Barry Schwartz, who popularised the term 'the paradox of choice', argued that excessive options impose a psychological cost, because each additional alternative raises expectations and increases the likelihood that people will regret the option they eventually select."}, {"l": "D", "t": "Schwartz drew a distinction between two types of decision-maker. 'Maximisers' feel compelled to examine every possibility in order to identify the best one, while 'satisficers' are content to select the first option that meets their criteria. In surveys, maximisers tend to achieve objectively better outcomes, for instance starting salaries that are higher on average, yet they report feeling less satisfied with those outcomes and more prone to depression. The constant awareness of alternatives, it seems, prevents them from enjoying what they have chosen."}, {"l": "E", "t": "In recent years, however, the idea of choice overload has itself come under scrutiny. When researchers attempted to replicate the original findings, the results were inconsistent. A comprehensive analysis that combined the data from fifty experiments found that, on average, the size of an assortment had virtually no effect on people's willingness to choose or their satisfaction. In some studies more options discouraged people; in others they had the opposite effect. This does not mean that choice overload is imaginary, the authors argued, but rather that it occurs only under particular conditions."}, {"l": "F", "t": "Identifying those conditions has become the focus of current research. Overload appears most likely when people lack clear preferences, when the options are difficult to compare, and when the decision must be made quickly. It is also more common when the alternatives are presented in a disorganised way. By contrast, experts who know exactly what they are looking for are rarely troubled by large assortments; a keen cyclist may welcome a shop with two hundred bicycles, while a novice may leave without buying anything. The same number of options can therefore be liberating for one person and paralysing for another."}, {"l": "G", "t": "These findings have obvious implications for businesses and governments. Online retailers increasingly use filters and recommendations to reduce the effective number of options a customer must consider, while still offering a large range. Some pension schemes now enrol employees automatically in a default fund, allowing those who wish to make an active choice to do so. Such 'choice architecture' has been praised for helping people make better decisions without restricting their freedom, though critics warn that it can also be used to steer consumers towards products that benefit the seller rather than the buyer."}, {"l": "H", "t": "The debate over the paradox of choice thus illustrates a broader lesson about psychological research. A striking result from a single experiment can quickly become accepted as a general truth, repeated in books, lectures and advertising campaigns. Only later, when careful attempts at replication have been made, does a more complicated picture emerge. Choice is neither simply good nor simply bad; its effects depend on who is choosing, what they are choosing between and how the options are presented."}], "groups": [{"type": "matchinfo", "instr": "Questions 27–31. Which paragraph contains the following information? Write the correct letter, A–H.", "opts": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E", "F", "G", "H"], "items": [{"q": "a summary of results from many different experiments", "a": "E", "loc": "E", "s": "A comprehensive analysis that combined the data from fifty experiments", "ex": "Мета-анализ 50 экспериментов.", "trap": "Абзац C тоже перечисляет исследования, но не объединяет данные."}, {"q": "a description of a strategy that automatically assigns people an option", "a": "G", "loc": "G", "s": "enrol employees automatically in a default fund", "ex": "Default fund = автоматический выбор.", "trap": "Пенсионные фонды упоминаются и в C — но там другой контекст."}, {"q": "an example showing that knowledge affects how people react to many options", "a": "F", "loc": "F", "s": "a keen cyclist may welcome a shop with two hundred bicycles", "ex": "Эксперт vs новичок.", "trap": "—"}, {"q": "precise figures comparing the behaviour of two groups of shoppers", "a": "B", "loc": "B", "s": "compared with just three per cent", "ex": "30% vs 3%.", "trap": "Абзац D тоже сравнивает две группы, но без точных цифр."}, {"q": "a warning that a technique might be used for unfair purposes", "a": "G", "loc": "G", "s": "it can also be used to steer consumers towards products that benefit the seller", "ex": "Критики предупреждают о манипуляции.", "trap": "Одна буква может использоваться дважды — это нормально в IELTS."}]}, {"type": "tfng", "instr": "Questions 32–35. Write TRUE, FALSE or NOT GIVEN.", "items": [{"q": "The larger jam display attracted fewer visitors than the smaller one.", "a": "FALSE", "loc": "B", "s": "The larger display attracted more visitors", "ex": "Больший выбор привлёк больше посетителей.", "trap": "Покупок было меньше, а посетителей — больше. Не путайте показатели."}, {"q": "Maximisers generally earn higher starting salaries than satisficers.", "a": "TRUE", "loc": "D", "s": "starting salaries that are higher on average", "ex": "Объективно лучшие результаты, напр. выше зарплаты.", "trap": "Они менее довольны — но вопрос о зарплате, а не об удовлетворённости."}, {"q": "Barry Schwartz carried out the jam experiment.", "a": "FALSE", "loc": "B", "s": "by the psychologists Sheena Iyengar and Mark Lepper", "ex": "Эксперимент провели Айенгар и Леппер; Шварц популяризировал термин.", "trap": "Смешение имён исследователей — частая ловушка."}, {"q": "Choice overload is more common among older people.", "a": "NOT GIVEN", "loc": "F", "s": "", "ex": "Возраст не упоминается среди условий.", "trap": "Условия перечислены, но возраста среди них нет."}]}, {"type": "mcq", "instr": "Questions 36–37. Choose the correct letter, A, B, C or D.", "items": [{"q": "According to paragraph F, choice overload is most likely when people", "opts": ["A are experts in the product.", "B have plenty of time.", "C have no clear preferences.", "D see well-organised options."], "a": "C", "loc": "F", "s": "when people lack clear preferences", "ex": "Lack clear preferences = no clear preferences.", "trap": "B и D — противоположности условиям из текста."}, {"q": "What is the writer's purpose in paragraph H?", "opts": ["A to reject all research on choice", "B to draw a general lesson about research findings", "C to recommend fewer products in shops", "D to praise the original jam study"], "a": "B", "loc": "H", "s": "illustrates a broader lesson about psychological research", "ex": "Broader lesson = general lesson.", "trap": "A — слишком категорично."}]}, {"type": "complete", "instr": "Questions 38–40. Complete the sentences. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage.", "limit": 2, "items": [{"q": "Schwartz believed each extra option increases expectations and the chance of ______.", "a": ["regret"], "loc": "C", "s": "increases the likelihood that people will regret", "ex": "Regret (сожаление) — в тексте глагол, в ответе существительное той же формы.", "trap": "«psychological cost» — слишком общее."}, {"q": "People who pick the first acceptable option are called ______.", "a": ["satisficers"], "loc": "D", "s": "'satisficers' are content to select the first option", "ex": "Прямой ответ.", "trap": "Maximisers — противоположный тип."}, {"q": "Using filters and defaults to guide decisions is known as choice ______.", "a": ["architecture"], "loc": "G", "s": "Such 'choice architecture'", "ex": "Прямой ответ.", "trap": "—"}]}]}];